"""Book 4 pass 2: full worked solutions, Chapters 20-41."""
S = {}
S[20] = {
1: "Idea: P(dBm) = 10 log(P/1 mW). 5 W = 5000 mW, 10 log 5000 = 37.0 dBm. 250 W gives 10 log 250,000 = 54.0 dBm. 2 kW gives 10 log 2×10⁶ = 63.0 dBm. Check: 1 W is 30 dBm, and 5, 250 and 2000 are 7.0, 24.0 and 33.0 dB above 1 W. The usual mistake is to forget the 30 dB between watts and milliwatts.",
2: "Idea: EIRP = Pout − Lfeed + Gant, all in decibels. 20 W = 43.0 dBm, so EIRP = 43.0 − 3 + 12 = 52.0 dBm, 158 W. 3 dB leaves half the power, so the cable delivers 10.0 W and turns 10.0 W into heat. Check: 10 W into a 12 dBi antenna is 10 × 15.8 = 158 W EIRP. The usual mistake is to add the antenna gain to the amplifier power before the cable loss and to forget that the lost half heats the cable.",
3: "Idea: a loss of L dB leaves 10^(−L/10) of the power. 10^(−0.2) = 0.631, so 63.1 % remains and 36.9 % becomes heat. Check: 3 dB leaves 50 %, and 2 dB must leave more. The usual mistake is to use 20 log, which gives 0.794 and describes voltage, not power.",
4: "Idea: match the device family to frequency, power and supply. At 3.5 GHz and 200 W, GaN-on-SiC is the choice: it works well above 3 GHz from 28 to 50 V with high power density and good heat removal through the silicon-carbide substrate. LDMOS is at or beyond its practical upper frequency there, and GaAs would need many devices and a large combiner. Check: 5G mid-band macro radios at 3.5 GHz are built on GaN, Chapter 45. The usual mistake is to pick LDMOS on cost alone at a frequency where its gain and efficiency have fallen.",
5: "Idea: losses in series add in decibels; divide the wanted output by the linear factor. 0.5 + 0.5 = 1.0 dB, a factor of 0.794, so the amplifier must deliver 40/0.794 = 50.4 W. Check: 50.4 W is 47.0 dBm, and 47.0 − 1.0 = 46.0 dBm = 40 W. The usual mistake is to multiply 40 W by 1.1 or by 1.26 without working in decibels.",
6: "Idea: Ropt = (VDD − Vknee)²/(2P) grows as the square of the supply at a given power. A higher supply means a smaller current and a higher optimum load, closer to 50 Ω, so the matching network needs a smaller ratio, a lower Q, a wider band and less loss. Check: Worked Example 21.1 found ratios of 4.9 at 50 V and 16 at 28 V for 100 W. The usual mistake is to think a higher voltage helps only by giving more power; at a fixed power it mostly helps the match.",
}
S[21] = {
1: "Idea: Ropt = (VDD − Vknee)²/(2P). At 50 V with a 5 V knee and 200 W, Ropt = 45²/400 = 5.06 Ω. At 28 V with a 3 V knee and 50 W, Ropt = 25²/100 = 6.25 Ω. Check: the 50 V device delivers four times the power at a slightly lower load, consistent with Ropt ∝ V²/P: (45/25)² × (50/200) = 0.81, and 6.25 × 0.81 = 5.06. The usual mistake is to use VDD in place of VDD − Vknee.",
2: "Idea: the peak fundamental current is 2P/(VDD − Vknee). 200/45 = 4.44 A. Check: P = ½ × 45 V × 4.44 A = 100 W. The usual mistake is to use the DC current, which in class B is the average of the half-sine current, (2/π) × 4.44 = 2.83 A, not the fundamental amplitude.",
3: "Idea: an L-section's Q is √(Rhigh/Rlow − 1). Q = √(50/3.125 − 1) = √15 = 3.87. Check: this is the 28 V design of Worked Example 21.1, whose ratio of 16 gives a Q almost twice the 50 V design's 1.98. The usual mistake is to stop at one section with a Q this high; two sections through 12.5 Ω would each have Q = 1.73.",
4: "Idea: the same load-line formula at handset voltages. Ropt = (3.4 − 0.3)²/(2 × 1) = 9.61/2 = 4.8 Ω. Check: the ratio to 50 Ω is 10.4, so a single L-section would need Q = 3.1. The usual mistake is to use the full-battery 4.4 V and find the amplifier compressing as the battery sags.",
5: "Idea: with a negligible knee, Ropt = VDD²/(2P), so doubling VDD at constant P multiplies Ropt by four. Check: 25 V to 50 V at 100 W takes Ropt from 3.125 to 12.5 Ω. The usual mistake is to say it doubles, confusing the load with the current, which halves.",
6: "Idea: the device's output resistance, hundreds of ohms in a large transistor, is the load a conjugate match would present. With that load the voltage would reach the knee and the supply long before the current reached its maximum, so the device would deliver a small fraction of its available power. The load-line match uses the full voltage and current ranges at once. Check: the difference measured by load-pull is typically 2 to 3 dB of output power. The usual mistake is to apply small-signal matching rules to a power stage.",
}
S[22] = {
1: "Idea: ηD = Pout/PDC, PAE = (Pout − Pin)/PDC, and Pdiss = PDC + Pin − Pout. ηD = 50/75 = 66.7 %. PAE = (50 − 1)/75 = 65.3 %. Pdiss = 75 + 1 − 50 = 26 W. Check: energy balance: 76 W in, 50 W out as RF, 26 W as heat. The usual mistake is to forget that the drive power also ends up as heat in the device.",
2: "Idea: η(α) = (α − sin α)/[4(sin(α/2) − (α/2)cos(α/2))]. α = 270° = 4.712 rad. Numerator 4.712 − (−1) = 5.712. Denominator 4 × (sin 135° − 2.356 cos 135°) = 4 × (0.7071 + 1.666) = 9.493. η = 5.712/9.493 = 60.2 %. Check: the result lies between class A's 50 % at 360° and class B's 78.5 % at 180°. The usual mistake is to work in degrees inside the α − sin α term.",
3: "Idea: in class C the device conducts for only part of the cycle, so a given drive produces a smaller fundamental current. Reaching full current needs a larger input swing, part of which only drives the device further below cutoff, and the gain falls. Check: at 120° the fundamental per unit peak current is about 0.39 against 0.5 for class B, and the drive needed rises further because the device is biased off. The usual mistake is to think class C's lower gain is a device limitation; it is a property of the bias.",
4: "Idea: PDC = Pout/η and the dissipation is the difference. PDC = 200/0.6 = 333.3 W, so 133.3 W is dissipated. Check: 60 % out and 40 % as heat, 0.4 × 333.3 = 133.3 W. The usual mistake is to compute 40 % of the output, 80 W, rather than of the input.",
5: "Idea: convert to watts and apply PAE = (Pout − Pin)/PDC. Pout = 1 W, Pin = 30 − 8 = 22 dBm = 0.158 W, PDC = 1/0.5 = 2 W. PAE = (1 − 0.158)/2 = 42.1 %. Check: with 8 dB of gain the drive is 16 % of the output, so PAE falls about 8 points below the 50 % drain efficiency. The usual mistake is to subtract decibels, 30 − 22, inside the PAE formula.",
6: "Idea: class E is defined by two conditions at the instant the switch closes: the switch voltage is zero, and its slope is zero. No charge is then dumped from the shunt capacitor through the switch, and small timing errors cost little. Check: Chapter 35's design equations are the ones that satisfy both conditions for a 50 % duty cycle. The usual mistake is to state only the zero-voltage condition; without the zero slope the design is sensitive to timing and component tolerance.",
}
S[23] = {
1: "Idea: OIP3 = Pout,tone + ΔIM3/2. OIP3 = 20 + 46/2 = 43 dBm. Check: P_IM3 = 3 × 20 − 2 × 43 = −26 dBm, which is 46 dB below each 20 dBm tone. The usual mistake is to add the whole 46 dB.",
2: "Idea: the tones fall 1 dB per decibel and the products 3 dB, so the ratio improves by 2 dB per decibel of back-off. 46 + 2 × 3 = 52 dBc. Check: P_IM3 = 3 × 17 − 2 × 43 = −35 dBm, and 17 − (−35) = 52 dB. The usual mistake is to improve the ratio by 3 dB per decibel, forgetting that the tones fall too.",
3: "Idea: for class B, η = (π/4)v with v the normalized amplitude, v = 10^(−BO/20). v = 10^(−0.5) = 0.316. η = 0.785 × 0.316 = 24.8 %. Check: at 6 dB it is 39.4 % and at 10 dB about 25 %, the steady fall of a fixed-supply stage. The usual mistake is to use 10^(−BO/10), which gives 7.9 %, class A's figure.",
4: "Idea: below 6 dB back-off the ideal symmetric Doherty behaves as a class B stage whose peak efficiency is reached at half amplitude: η = (π/4)(v/0.5). v = 0.316, so η = 0.785 × 0.632 = 49.7 %. Check: exactly twice Problem 3's class B figure. The usual mistake is to use 78.5 % at all back-offs; the Doherty holds that figure only down to 6 dB.",
5: "Idea: the average output is the peak reduced by the PAPR, and the DC power is the average divided by the average efficiency. 200 × 10^(−0.9) = 25.2 W average. PDC = 25.2/0.55 = 45.8 W, and 20.6 W is dissipated. Check: an ideal class B at 9 dB back-off would be 27.9 % efficient and draw 90 W. The usual mistake is to divide the peak power by the efficiency.",
6: "Idea: the fundamental output rises 1 dB per decibel of input, while third-order products, proportional to the cube of the input amplitude, rise 3 dB. Backing off by 1 dB lowers the tones 1 dB and the products 3 dB, widening the ratio by 2 dB. Check: Problem 2. The usual mistake is to expect the rule to hold near compression, where higher orders bend the curves.",
}
S[24] = {
1: "Idea: Tj = Tambient + Pdiss θtotal. Tj = 45 + 30 × 2.5 = 120 °C. Check: the rise of 75 °C is far below a GaN part's 225 °C rating but would matter for a silicon part rated at 150 °C on a hotter day. The usual mistake is to use the case temperature as the ambient.",
2: "Idea: two equal signals with phase error θ combine to a fraction cos²(θ/2) of their ideal sum. cos²(7.5°) = 0.9914² = 98.3 %. Check: Section 24.2 gave 99.2 % at 10° and 97.0 % at 20°, and 15° falls between. The usual mistake is to use cos²θ, which gives 93.3 %.",
3: "Idea: with one of N inputs dead, the ideal output falls to ((N − 1)/N)² of full power. (7/8)² = 0.766, a fall of 10 log 0.766 = −1.16 dB. Check: Section 24.2 gives 77 % for N = 8. The usual mistake is to expect 7/8, −0.58 dB, forgetting that the combiner also dumps part of the survivors' power.",
4: "Idea: |Γ| = (VSWR − 1)/(VSWR + 1), reflected fraction |Γ|². |Γ| = 0.5/2.5 = 0.2, so 4.0 % is reflected. Check: the return loss is 14.0 dB. The usual mistake is to think a 1.5 VSWR is serious; it reflects only 4 % and costs 0.18 dB.",
5: "Idea: cable loss scales with length in decibels. 45 × 6.6/100 = 2.97 dB. 40 × 10^(−0.297) = 20.2 W at the antenna. Check: close to 3 dB, so close to half, 20 W. The usual mistake is to scale the power linearly with length.",
6: "Idea: in normal operation the isolation resistors absorb only the small imbalance between the inputs. When a device fails, they absorb a large part of the surviving devices' power: for N = 4 with one failed, 75 W of 300 W in Worked Example 24.2. They must be rated for that, continuously, until the fault is cleared. Check: (N − 1)P − (N − 1)²P/N = 300 − 225 = 75 W. The usual mistake is to rate them for normal imbalance and lose them, and the combiner, on the first device failure.",
}
S[25] = {
1: "Idea: Te = T0(F − 1). F = 10^0.2 = 1.585. Te = 290 × 0.585 = 170 K. Check: Chapter 17's Problem 1 found 169.6 K. The usual mistake is to use 2 directly as the noise factor.",
2: "Idea: Friis: F = F1 + (F2 − 1)/G1. F1 = 10^0.08 = 1.202, F2 = 10^1.2 = 15.85, G1 = 100. F = 1.202 + 14.85/100 = 1.351, a noise figure of 1.30 dB. Check: the second stage adds only half a decibel despite its 12 dB noise figure, because 20 dB of gain shields it. The usual mistake is to add the noise figures in decibels.",
3: "Idea: Nfloor = −174 dBm/Hz + 10 log B + NF. 10 log(2×10⁷) = 73.0 dB. Nfloor = −174 + 73.0 + 2 = −99.0 dBm. Check: Worked Example 34.3's 20 MHz receiver with 5 dB gave −96 dBm, 3 dB higher. The usual mistake is to use 20 log for bandwidth.",
4: "Idea: SFDR = (2/3)(IIP3 − Nfloor). SFDR = (2/3)(0 − (−99.0)) = 66.0 dB. Check: at the top of the range the third-order products equal the floor: input tones at −99 + 66 = −33 dBm give products at 3(−33) − 2(0) = −99 dBm. The usual mistake is to use the full IIP3-to-floor span, 99 dB.",
5: "Idea: reciprocal mixing moves the oscillator's noise skirt onto the blocker, giving a noise of Pblocker + L(Δf) + 10 log B. −30 − 120 + 60 = −90 dBm. Check: this is 9 dB above Problem 3's −99 dBm floor and would dominate it; the oscillator, not the LNA, sets the sensitivity in the presence of this blocker. The usual mistake is to forget the bandwidth term.",
6: "Idea: a passive loss at T0 attenuates the signal but still delivers kT0B of noise at its output, so the SNR falls by the full loss. Nothing ahead of it provides gain to make its contribution small, so its noise figure, equal to its loss, adds directly. Check: Worked Example 25.1's filter-first case lost 1.5 dB of sensitivity. The usual mistake is to think a low-loss filter is noise-free.",
}
S[26] = {
1: "Idea: inside the loop bandwidth the reference's phase noise is multiplied by N, which is 20 log N in decibels. 20 log 145 = 43.2 dB. Check: N = 100 would be 40 dB and N = 200 46 dB. The usual mistake is to use 10 log N; phase deviations multiply, so their power rises as N².",
2: "Idea: in Leeson's model every term is proportional to 2FkT/Ps, so doubling the resonator power Ps lowers the phase noise by 3 dB at all offsets where the model holds. Check: the flat far-out floor −161 dBc/Hz of Worked Example 26.1 becomes −164 dBc/Hz at 2 mW. The usual mistake is to expect 6 dB; it is a power ratio.",
3: "Idea: σφ = √(2∫L df) with L linear, and jitter = σφ/(2πf0). 2 × 10⁻¹⁰ × (10⁶ − 10³) = 2.0×10⁻⁴ rad², so σφ = 0.0141 rad = 0.81°. Jitter = 0.0141/(2π × 10⁹) = 2.25 ps rms. Check: Worked Example 26.3's −110 dBc/Hz over ten times the band gave the same σφ, and the jitter here is 2.4 times larger because the carrier is 2.4 times lower. The usual mistake is to omit the factor 2 for the two sidebands.",
4: "Idea: N = fout/fref, and the in-band noise is Lref + 20 log N. N = 5800/40 = 145. In-band noise = −150 + 43.2 = −106.8 dBc/Hz. Check: Problem 1. The usual mistake is to forget that a fractional-N design at 40 MHz still multiplies by about 145; fine steps come from the fractional part, not from a lower N.",
5: "Idea: the settling time is about −ln(ε√(1 − ζ²))/(ζωn), inversely proportional to fn at fixed ζ. Halving fn from 100 kHz to 50 kHz doubles 31.9 µs to 63.8 µs. Check: −ln(10⁻⁶ × 0.707) = 14.16, and 14.16/(0.707 × 2π × 50,000) = 63.8 µs. The usual mistake is to narrow the loop for noise and forget the channel-switching time.",
6: "Idea: the divider makes the loop multiply the reference's phase by N, so every reference phase deviation appears N times larger at the output, raising its noise by 20 log N. A smaller N, which needs a higher reference frequency at the detector, multiplies it less. Check: Worked Example 26.2 gained 32 dB by moving from N = 2400 to N ≈ 60. The usual mistake is to blame the VCO for in-band noise, which inside the loop bandwidth comes from the reference and detector.",
}
S[27] = {
1: "Idea: the radar equation gives Pr ∝ Pt/R⁴, so the maximum range scales as Pt^(1/4). Doubling the range needs 2⁴ = 16 times the power, 12 dB. Check: Worked Example 27.1 found that doubling the power extends the range only by 2^(1/4) = 1.19. The usual mistake is to use the one-way inverse-square law and answer 4.",
2: "Idea: range resolution is c/(2B), set by bandwidth, not by pulse length. c/(2 × 150×10⁶) = 1.0 m. Check: Worked Example 27.2's 10 MHz chirp gave 15 m, and fifteen times the bandwidth gives a fifteenth of it. The usual mistake is to forget the factor 2 for the round trip.",
3: "Idea: an echo must return before the next pulse leaves: Runamb = c × PRI/2. 2.998×10⁸ × 500×10⁻⁶/2 = 75 km. Check: 1 ms gave 150 km in Worked Example 27.2. The usual mistake is to use the pulse repetition frequency in place of the interval.",
4: "Idea: average power is peak power times duty cycle, τ/PRI. Duty = 1 µs/1 ms = 0.001, so Pavg = 1 kW × 0.001 = 1 W. Check: the energy per pulse is 1 mJ, a thousand times a second. The usual mistake is to size the power supply and heat sink for the peak.",
5: "Idea: fd = 2v/λ. λ = 2.998×10⁸/77×10⁹ = 3.89 mm. fd = 2 × 30/0.00389 = 15.4 kHz. Check: Worked Example 27.3 found the same; at 10 GHz the same target would give only 2.0 kHz. The usual mistake is to use v/λ, the one-way shift.",
6: "Idea: the slope S = B/Tc and the beat frequency fb = 2RS/c. S = 4×10⁹/40×10⁻⁶ = 10¹⁴ Hz/s. fb = 2 × 100 × 10¹⁴/2.998×10⁸ = 66.7 MHz. Check: the range resolution of this sweep is 3.75 cm, and 100 m is 2667 resolution cells; a beat frequency this high needs a fast ADC, which Chapter 49 discusses. The usual mistake is to use the sweep time alone, without the bandwidth.",
}
S[28] = {
1: "Idea: F = Fmin + (4Rn/Z0)|Γs − Γopt|²/[(1 − |Γs|²)|1 + Γopt|²], with Γs = 0 for a 50 Ω source. Fmin = 10^0.1 = 1.259. 4Rn/Z0 = 0.8. |Γs − Γopt|² = 0.16 and |1 + j0.4|² = 1.16. F = 1.259 + 0.8 × 0.16/1.16 = 1.259 + 0.110 = 1.369, or 1.36 dB. Check: the penalty for skipping the noise match is 0.36 dB here, against 0.34 dB in Worked Example 28.1 with a smaller Rn and a larger |Γopt|. The usual mistake is to forget the |1 + Γopt|² term.",
2: "Idea: loss before the LNA multiplies the noise factor of everything after it: F = L × (F1 + (F2 − 1)/G1). L = 10^0.05 = 1.122, F1 = 1.259, F2 = 10, G1 = 100. F = 1.122 × (1.259 + 0.09) = 1.122 × 1.349 = 1.514, or 1.80 dB. Check: 0.5 dB of loss plus 1.30 dB for the LNA and second stage gives 1.80 dB. The usual mistake is to apply the 0.5 dB after the LNA.",
3: "Idea: an inductance Ls in the source of a FET whose gate sees its own capacitance Cgs produces an input impedance with a real part ωT Ls, made by the device's transconductance, not by a resistor. That real part is noiseless, so it moves the input match toward the conjugate of the device's input without adding noise. Check: with fT = 50 GHz and Ls = 0.16 nH, ωT Ls = 2π × 50×10⁹ × 0.16×10⁻⁹ = 50 Ω. The usual mistake is to think the 50 Ω is a physical resistor; a real resistor there would add noise.",
4: "Idea: the second stage adds (F2 − 1)/G1 to the noise factor. F2 = 10^1.2 = 15.85, G1 = 10^1.5 = 31.6. (15.85 − 1)/31.6 = 0.47. Check: with F1 = 1.26 the total would be 1.73, or 2.4 dB; the second stage dominates. The usual mistake is to use the gain in decibels in the denominator.",
5: "Idea: the system noise figure includes the following stages divided by the first stage's gain. A lower-noise device with less gain divides them by a smaller number, and the system can get worse. Check: Worked Example 28.2's device B, 0.6 dB with 6 dB of gain, gave 2.78 dB, against 1.76 dB for device A at 0.8 dB with 10 dB of gain. The usual mistake is to choose the LNA from its own noise figure alone.",
6: "Idea: each half of a balanced LNA is driven through a 90° hybrid. The reflections from the two identical halves return through the same hybrid with a further 90°, so they arrive at the input port 180° apart and cancel, and at the isolated port in phase, where the load absorbs them. Each half can therefore be noise-matched while the pair presents a good input match. Check: the cost is the hybrid's loss ahead of the devices, which adds to the noise figure, and twice the devices. The usual mistake is to think the hybrid improves the noise; it only fixes the match.",
}
S[29] = {
1: "Idea: with a high-side LO, fLO = fRF + fIF and the image is fLO + fIF = fRF + 2fIF. fLO = 5.8 + 1.0 = 6.8 GHz, image 6.8 + 1.0 = 7.8 GHz. Check: the image is 2 GHz from the wanted signal, twice the IF. The usual mistake is to put the image at fLO − fIF, which is the wanted signal.",
2: "Idea: the half-IF spur, m = n = 2, lies halfway between the wanted signal and the LO: fRF ± fIF/2 toward the LO. fLO = 900 − 70 = 830 MHz; the spur is at 900 − 35 = 865 MHz. Check: 2 × 865 − 2 × 830 = 70 MHz, the IF. The usual mistake is to look for it on the far side of the wanted signal, where the LO is not.",
3: "Idea: the image must be rejected by its excess over the wanted signal plus the margin required. 30 + 15 = 45 dB. Check: after 45 dB the interferer is at −15 dB relative to the wanted signal. The usual mistake is to subtract the two numbers.",
4: "Idea: a passive mixer with conversion loss L behaves like an attenuator at about room temperature, so its noise figure is about equal to its loss, here about 6.5 dB, plus a little from the LO's noise and the switches. Check: datasheets for passive diode mixers list noise figures within a few tenths of a decibel of the conversion loss for single-sideband measurements. The usual mistake is to assume a mixer adds no noise because it has no gain.",
5: "Idea: the image lies 2fIF from the wanted signal. A higher IF moves it farther, where the front-end filter's skirt attenuates it more easily. Check: Worked Example 29.1's 400 MHz IF put the image 800 MHz away; a 40 MHz IF would put it 80 MHz away. The usual mistake is to forget the cost: a high IF makes the channel filter at the IF harder.",
6: "Idea: in a direct-conversion transmitter the LO is at the carrier, so any leakage lands in the middle of the transmitted channel, where it raises EVM and cannot be filtered. It must be nulled by trimming DC offsets, and kept low by layout and shielding. Check: a leak 30 dB below the signal alone limits EVM to about −30 dB, 3.2 %. The usual mistake is to treat LO leakage as an out-of-band emission that the harmonic filter will remove.",
7: "Idea: multiplying by a square wave of amplitude ±1 is a fundamental of 4/π, which puts (2/π) of the RF voltage into each of the sum and difference products. The fraction of power at the wanted IF is (2/π)² = 0.405, a conversion loss of 20 log(π/2) = 3.92 dB. Check: real diode mixers show 6 to 7 dB because of diode resistance, baluns and imperfect switching. The usual mistake is to expect 6 dB from splitting into two sidebands; the ideal switch does better.",
8: "Idea: Section 29.4's rule of thumb puts a diode mixer's IIP3 about 10 dB above its LO drive. With +7 dBm of LO, about +17 dBm. Check: level-7 mixers are listed with input intercepts in the mid-teens of dBm, and higher-level mixers with +13 or +17 dBm of LO reach +20 dBm and more. The usual mistake is to raise the RF level instead of the LO level when more linearity is needed.",
}
S[30] = {
1: "Idea: Lpath = 20 log(4πd f/c). 4π × 2000 × 24×10⁹/2.998×10⁸ = 2.01×10⁶, so 20 log = 126.1 dB. Check: Worked Example 30.1's 10 km at 5.8 GHz gave 127.7 dB; a fifth of the distance (−14.0 dB) at 4.14 times the frequency (+12.3 dB) gives 126.0 dB. The usual mistake is to mix units, kilometers with gigahertz, in a formula written for meters and hertz.",
2: "Idea: Pr = Pt + Gt − Lpath + Gr. 20 + 30 − 120 + 30 = −40 dBm. Check: 60 dB of antenna gain recovers half of the 120 dB of path loss. The usual mistake is to subtract the receiving antenna's gain.",
3: "Idea: C/N0 = EIRP − Lpath + G/T − k, and C/N = C/N0 − 10 log B. Lpath = 20 log(4π × 3.6×10⁷ × 2×10¹⁰/2.998×10⁸) = 209.6 dB. C/N0 = 50 − 209.6 + 10 + 228.6 = 79.0 dBHz. C/N = 79.0 − 70.0 = 9.0 dB. Check: Worked Example 30.2's Ku link reached 90.0 dBHz with more G/T and a lower frequency. The usual mistake is to subtract Boltzmann's −228.6 rather than add 228.6.",
4: "Idea: Te = 290(F − 1). F = 10^0.15 = 1.413. Te = 290 × 0.413 = 119.6 K. Check: between 1 dB (75 K) and 2 dB (170 K). The usual mistake is to forget the minus one and quote 410 K.",
5: "Idea: free-space loss grows 20 dB per decade of distance, so a factor of 3 adds 20 log 3 = 9.5 dB. Check: doubling adds 6.0 dB, as Worked Example 30.1 found. The usual mistake is to use 10 log, 4.8 dB.",
6: "Idea: the received C/N0 depends on the receiving antenna's gain and the system noise temperature only through their ratio, G/T, so one number describes the whole receiving station. A larger dish and a quieter LNA are interchangeable ways to raise it. Check: Worked Example 30.3 showed that 1.4 dB of lower Tsys equals a 17 % larger dish. The usual mistake is to compare stations by dish size alone.",
}
S[31] = {
1: "Idea: |Γ| = (s − 1)/(s + 1), RL = −20 log|Γ|, reflected fraction |Γ|². |Γ| = 1/3 = 0.333, RL = 9.5 dB, 11.1 % reflected. Check: Chapter 15's Problem 2. The usual mistake is to read 2:1 as 50 % reflected.",
2: "Idea: the matched peak voltage is √(2PR), and the worst-case peak on a mismatched line is (1 + |Γ|) times it. √(2 × 50 × 50) = 70.7 V. |Γ| = 4/6 = 0.667, so the worst case is 70.7 × 1.667 = 117.9 V. Check: at 10:1 Worked Example 31.2 found 1.818 times the matched value. The usual mistake is to multiply by the VSWR, 5, which gives 354 V and overdesigns the device.",
3: "Idea: the delivered fraction is 1 − |Γ|². |Γ| = 2/4 = 0.5, so 75 % reaches the load, −1.25 dB. Check: the return loss is 6.0 dB. The usual mistake is to think a 3:1 VSWR loses two thirds of the power.",
4: "Idea: a depletion-mode GaN HEMT is on at zero gate voltage. Powering the drain first lets an uncontrolled current flow, limited only by the supply and the circuit, which can destroy the device in microseconds. The gate must be pulled negative first, and on shutdown the drain removed first. Check: Chapter 45's bias-sequencing note shows a circuit that enforces the order. The usual mistake is to rely on a power-up order that the supplies happen to have on the bench.",
5: "Idea: a coupler's directivity sets how much forward power leaks into the reverse port. A reflection weaker than that leakage cannot be distinguished from it, so directivity limits the smallest mismatch that can be measured. Check: Worked Example 40.2: with 25 dB of directivity a 10 dB return loss is read within about ±0.16 dB, but a 25 dB return loss would be uncertain by several decibels. The usual mistake is to buy a coupler for its coupling value and ignore its directivity.",
6: "Idea: a bipolar device's base-emitter voltage falls about 2 mV per kelvin at constant current, so a hot cell draws more current, heats more, and hogs the current of its neighbors. An emitter ballast resistor drops more voltage as the current rises, providing negative feedback that holds each cell's current. Check: Worked Example 31.1's 10 K rise doubled the current of an unballasted stage; Chapter 44 sizes a ballast for a GaAs HBT. The usual mistake is to make the ballast too small to beat the thermal feedback, or so large that it costs output power.",
}
S[32] = {
1: "Idea: beam power is VI and perveance K = I/V^(3/2). 20 kV × 2 A = 40 kW. K = 2/(2×10⁴)^1.5 = 2/2.83×10⁶ = 0.71×10⁻⁶, 0.71 µperv. Check: within the 0.5 to 2 µperv typical of guns. The usual mistake is to take the square root instead of the 3/2 power.",
2: "Idea: γ = 1 + eV/(mc²) and v = c√(1 − 1/γ²). γ = 1 + 20/511 = 1.039. 1/γ² = 0.926, so v = c√0.074 = 0.272c. Check: at 10 kV Worked Example 32.1 found 0.195c, and doubling the voltage raises v by about √2 at these energies, to 0.276c, a little less because of relativity. The usual mistake is to use the classical ½mv² = eV, which gives 0.280c.",
3: "Idea: PDC = Pout/η and the heat is the difference. 1000/0.7 = 1429 W in, 429 W of heat. Check: 70 % out, 30 % heat: 0.3 × 1429 = 429 W. The usual mistake is to compute 30 % of the output.",
4: "Idea: a klystron exchanges energy with the beam through resonant cavities, whose bandwidth is narrow. A helix TWT exchanges energy along a nonresonant slow-wave structure whose phase velocity is nearly constant over a wide band, so it stays synchronized with the beam over an octave or more. Check: Section 32.3 lists octave bandwidths for helix TWTs against a few percent for klystrons. The usual mistake is to think the TWT's bandwidth comes from its gain.",
5: "Idea: a depressed collector is held below the beam's accelerating voltage, so the spent electrons are slowed by a retarding field before they land. Energy they would have dumped as heat returns to the supply, and the efficiency rises. Check: Worked Example 32.2 recovered 60 % of 3 kW and raised the efficiency from 40 % to 62.5 %. The usual mistake is to depress the collector so far that slow electrons are turned back into the interaction region.",
6: "Idea: combined power is the sum minus the combining loss. 16 × 150 W = 2400 W, and 0.5 dB leaves 10^(−0.05) = 0.891, so 2.14 kW. Check: the 0.26 kW lost is the price of the combiner tree. The usual mistake is to use 20 log for the loss factor.",
}
S[33] = {
1: "Idea: 1 mW/cm² is 10⁻³ W per 10⁻⁴ m², which is 10 W/m². Check: this is the FCC general-public limit above 1.5 GHz. The usual mistake is to convert cm² to m² with a factor of 100 instead of 10,000.",
2: "Idea: between 300 and 1500 MHz the FCC general-public limit is f/1500 mW/cm² with f in megahertz. 600/1500 = 0.4 mW/cm² = 4 W/m². Check: at 1500 MHz the formula reaches 1 mW/cm², joining the flat limit above. The usual mistake is to use the 10 W/m² limit at all frequencies.",
3: "Idea: far-field power density S = EIRP/(4πR²). 50/(4π × 9) = 0.44 W/m². Check: far below 10 W/m², so compliance is met at 3 m in the main beam even with the 2.56 reflection factor (1.13 W/m²). The usual mistake is to use the transmitter power when the antenna has gain.",
4: "Idea: R = √(EIRP/(4πSlimit)). At 28 GHz the limit is 10 W/m². R = √(1000/(4π × 10)) = 2.82 m. Check: S = 1000/(4π × 2.82²) = 10.0 W/m². The usual mistake is to forget that a millimeter-wave array's beam moves, so the time-averaged exposure in any direction is usually much less.",
5: "Idea: in the far field S = E²/η0 with η0 = 376.7 Ω. E = √(10 × 376.7) = 61.4 V/m rms. Check: 61.4²/377 = 10.0 W/m². The usual mistake is to use E²/(2η0), which applies to peak, not rms, field.",
6: "Idea: a phone at the head puts the tissue in the antenna's reactive near field, where power density is not well defined and energy couples directly into the tissue. What matters biologically there is the power absorbed per kilogram, the SAR. Check: 1.6 W/kg over 1 g would warm tissue by about 0.027 K per minute without blood flow, Section 33.3. The usual mistake is to apply the far-field formula at a few millimeters.",
}
S[34] = {
1: "Idea: the receiver's noise floor density is kT0 plus its noise figure: −174 + NF dBm/Hz. −174 + 4 = −170 dBm/Hz. Check: in a 1 MHz channel that is −110 dBm. The usual mistake is to forget that the floor density is quoted per hertz, before any bandwidth is added.",
2: "Idea: a leak at a given level below the floor raises it by 10 log(1 + 10^(ΔdB/10)). 10 log(1 + 10^(−0.6)) = 10 log 1.251 = 0.97 dB. Check: at 9 dB below, Worked Example 34.1 found 0.51 dB, and at 0 dB it would be 3 dB. The usual mistake is to think a leak below the floor costs nothing.",
3: "Idea: full duplex must cancel the transmitter down to the receiver's noise floor. Floor = −174 + 10 log(10⁷) + 4 = −174 + 70 + 4 = −100 dBm. Cancellation = 23 − (−100) = 123 dB. Check: Worked Example 34.3's 25 dBm in 20 MHz with 5 dB gave 121 dB. The usual mistake is to stop at the receiver's sensitivity, which would leave the self-interference at the level of the wanted signal.",
4: "Idea: the blocker at the LNA is the transmit power minus the duplexer's isolation in the transmit band. 28 − 55 = −27 dBm. Check: the LNA's input compression point must be comfortably above −27 dBm, 10 dB or more, as Worked Example 34.2 argued. The usual mistake is to use the receive-band isolation, which governs the noise, not the carrier.",
5: "Idea: in TDD the receiver is not listening while the transmitter is on. Only the switch's isolation and its settling between bursts matter, not continuous isolation during transmission. Check: Wi-Fi and 5G TDD radios use a T/R switch of a few tens of decibels of isolation where an FDD handset needs a duplexer of 50 dB and more. The usual mistake is to forget the guard time the switch needs, which costs throughput.",
6: "Idea: the transmitter's noise in the receive band lies at the same frequencies as the wanted signal. Any filter in the receiver that removed it would remove the signal too, so it has to be stopped before it reaches the receiver, by the duplexer's receive-band rejection on the transmit side. Check: Worked Example 34.1 sets that isolation from the noise level and the receiver floor. The usual mistake is to look for the cure in the receiver's channel filter.",
}
S[35] = {
1: "Idea: class E design equations: R = 0.5768 VDD²/P and C = 0.1836/(ωR). R = 0.5768 × 144/10 = 8.31 Ω. C = 0.1836/(2π × 13.56×10⁶ × 8.31) = 259 pF. Check: at 13.56 MHz the capacitor's reactance is 45.3 Ω, 5.45 R, the class E ratio 1/0.1836. The usual mistake is to forget the 2π and get a capacitance six times too large.",
2: "Idea: the class E switch peak is 3.562 VDD. 3.562 × 12 = 42.7 V. Check: a 60 V-rated MOSFET leaves about 30 % margin, the usual choice for 12 V class E. The usual mistake is to pick a 40 V part because the supply is only 12 V.",
3: "Idea: full-power class E is possible only while the device's output capacitance does not exceed the required shunt C: fmax = 0.1836/(2πR Cout). 0.1836/(2π × 4.52 × 5×10⁻¹²) = 1293 MHz. Check: Worked Example 35.2 found 646 MHz for 10 pF, and half the capacitance doubles it. The usual mistake is to forget that package and layout add capacitance to the data sheet's Cout.",
4: "Idea: η = ½(V1/VDD)(I1/IDC). With the voltage limited to the fundamental, V1/VDD = 1, and a half-sine current has I1/IDC = π/2. η = ½ × 1 × 1.571 = 78.5 %, the class B value. Check: class F's gain over class B comes entirely from the voltage harmonics that raise V1 above VDD, Worked Example 35.3. The usual mistake is to credit harmonic tuning with efficiency when only the current is shaped.",
5: "Idea: class E's switch voltage peaks at about 3.6 times the supply. A device with high breakdown voltage tolerates that swing at a supply high enough to give a useful load resistance and power. GaN, with breakdown voltages of 100 V and more at 28 V supplies, fits; thin-oxide CMOS needs stacking, Chapter 42. Check: Worked Example 35.1's 100 W design at 28 V peaks at 99.7 V. The usual mistake is to choose the device for the supply voltage, not the peak.",
6: "Idea: each added harmonic contributes less to the fundamental's amplitude than the one before: with the third harmonic V1/VDD reaches 1.155, and the full square wave only 1.273. The first two or three harmonics do most of the shaping, and the device's output capacitance shorts the higher ones anyway. Check: Worked Example 35.3 reached 81.6 % with the second and third harmonics, and 90.7 % with a full half-sine current; the ideal is 100 %. The usual mistake is to add harmonic traps whose loss costs more than the harmonic gains.",
}
S[36] = {
1: "Idea: class B efficiency at back-off is (π/4) × 10^(−BO/20). 78.5 % × 10^(−0.5) = 78.5 % × 0.316 = 24.8 %. Check: Chapter 23's Problem 3 gave the same number. The usual mistake is to divide by the power ratio, 10, instead of the voltage ratio, 3.16.",
2: "Idea: the efficiencies of cascaded energy conversions multiply. 0.70 × 0.80 = 0.56, 56 %. Check: the fixed-supply alternative at 8 dB back-off is 31 %, so the tracker gains 25 points. The usual mistake is to average the two efficiencies.",
3: "Idea: outphasing output power is Pmax cos²φ. cos²φ = 0.5 gives cos φ = 0.707 and φ = 45°. Check: Worked Example 36.3 found 44.9° for 3 dB. The usual mistake is to set cos φ = 0.5, which is 6 dB.",
4: "Idea: the envelope's bandwidth is about three to five times the signal's. For 20 MHz, about 60 to 100 MHz. Check: Worked Example 36.2 found 300 to 500 MHz for 100 MHz. The usual mistake is to use the signal bandwidth, which leaves the fast envelope transitions unfollowed and distorts the peaks.",
5: "Idea: in a polar transmitter all the amplitude information passes through the supply. The envelope is several times wider than the signal, the amplitude and phase paths must be aligned to a small fraction of the symbol time, and the amplitude path must reach down to zero cleanly. Efficient supply modulators cannot follow a wideband envelope. Check: for 100 MHz OFDM the envelope needs several hundred megahertz, Section 36.3. The usual mistake is to assume an ET tracker's bandwidth is enough; ET keeps the RF path linear, polar does not.",
6: "Idea: at back-off the two outphased amplifiers' outputs partly cancel. An isolating combiner turns the cancelled power into heat in its isolation resistor, so each amplifier keeps delivering full power and the efficiency falls with output. A non-isolating Chireix combiner instead changes each amplifier's load, reducing its current. Check: at 6 dB back-off with an isolating combiner, three quarters of the amplifiers' power is dissipated. The usual mistake is to think outphasing is efficient regardless of the combiner.",
}
S[37] = {
1: "Idea: the load-line starting point, Ropt = (VDD − Vknee)²/(2P). (48 − 4)²/(2 × 50) = 1936/100 = 19.4 Ω. Check: Chapter 45's Doherty uses the same 48 V and 4 V knee. The usual mistake is to treat this as the final load; load-pull moves it.",
2: "Idea: PAE = (Pout − Pin)/PDC. Pin = 20 W/10 = 2 W. PAE = (20 − 2)/32 = 56.3 %, against a drain efficiency of 62.5 %. Check: 10 dB of gain costs a tenth of the output, 6.2 points. The usual mistake is to subtract the gain in decibels from the efficiency.",
3: "Idea: at n dB of compression the output amplitude is 10^(−n/20) of the uncompressed value, so the error is 1 − 10^(−n/20). 1 − 10^(−0.1) = 1 − 0.794 = 20.6 %. Check: 1 dB gave 10.9 % in Worked Example 37.3. The usual mistake is to use 10^(−n/10), the power ratio.",
4: "Idea: P_IM3 = 3Pout − 2 OIP3 per tone. 3 × 25 − 2 × 45 = −15 dBm, 40 dB below each tone. Check: OIP3 − Pout = 20 dB, and the ratio is twice that. The usual mistake is to use the sum of the two tone powers.",
5: "Idea: ACLR is the main channel's power over the adjacent channel's, in decibels a difference. 30 − (−16) = 46 dB. Check: 1 dB above the 45 dB that 3GPP asks of an NR base station. The usual mistake is to measure the two channels with different integration bandwidths.",
6: "Idea: the IM3 ratio improves 2 dB per decibel of back-off. 3 dB of back-off gives 6 dB. Check: Problem 4's tones at 22 dBm would give products at −24 dBm, 46 dB down. The usual mistake is to expect 9 dB, the fall of the products alone.",
}
S[38] = {
1: "Idea: a memory polynomial has K × M complex coefficients. 7 × 3 = 21. Check: with odd orders only, k = 1, 3, 5, 7, it would have 4 × 3 = 12. The usual mistake is to count real numbers; each coefficient is complex.",
2: "Idea: fifth-order distortion spreads over about five times the signal bandwidth. 5 × 20 MHz = 100 MHz. Check: Worked Example 38.2's 100 MHz carrier needed 500 MHz. The usual mistake is to size the observation receiver for the signal alone, which hides the distortion it must see.",
3: "Idea: class B efficiency is (π/4) × 10^(−BO/20). 0.785 × 10^(−0.4) = 0.785 × 0.398 = 31.3 %. Check: the same as Chapter 23's 8 dB figure. The usual mistake is to use 10^(−0.8).",
4: "Idea: at no back-off v = 1, so η = π/4 = 78.5 %. Check: this is the ideal peak of class B. The usual mistake is to expect a modulated signal ever to sit there; only its peaks do.",
5: "Idea: a predistorter changes only the input. The amplifier's maximum output is fixed by its supply, knee and device size, so a peak beyond saturation cannot be restored however much the input is expanded. The peaks must fit below saturation, usually with crest-factor reduction. Check: driving harder near saturation only adds distortion. The usual mistake is to expect DPD to extend P1dB.",
6: "Idea: memory arises wherever the amplifier's state depends on recent signal history. Two causes: the bias network's impedance at envelope frequencies, which lets the supply or gate voltage follow the envelope, and thermal changes in the device as the dissipation varies; charge trapping in GaN is a third. Check: each produces distortion that depends on the past signal and so cannot be fixed by a memoryless table. The usual mistake is to blame all memory on the matching network.",
}
S[39] = {
1: "Idea: a fractional-N synthesizer's step is fref/2^k. 50×10⁶/2²⁰ = 47.7 Hz. Check: Worked Example 39.1's 24-bit accumulator at 40 MHz gave 2.38 Hz. The usual mistake is to forget that the output step is the detector frequency divided by 2^k, which may differ from the crystal if a reference divider is used.",
2: "Idea: N + F/2^k = fout/fref. 5800/50 = 116 exactly, so N = 116 and F = 0. Check: an integer ratio puts the fractional accumulator at zero, which also avoids fractional spurs at this channel. The usual mistake is to look for a fractional part where there is none.",
3: "Idea: L = FOM + 10 log f_PFD + 20 log N. −229 + 80.0 + 35.3 = −113.7 dBc/Hz. Check: 20 log 58 = 35.27. The usual mistake is to use 20 log for the detector frequency.",
4: "Idea: the DDS step is fclk/2^n. 2.5×10⁹/2⁴⁸ = 8.9×10⁻⁶ Hz, about 8.9 µHz. Check: 2⁴⁸ = 2.81×10¹⁴. The usual mistake is to think the step is the useful resolution; spurs and the clock's stability limit the frequency accuracy long before.",
5: "Idea: truncating the phase to P bits gives spurs about −6.02P dBc. −6.02 × 12 = −72 dBc. Check: Worked Example 39.3's 14 bits gave −84 dBc. The usual mistake is to use the DAC's bit count, which sets a different limit.",
6: "Idea: at fixed fout, doubling f_PFD halves N, so 10 log f rises by 3 dB while 20 log N falls by 6 dB: a net 3 dB improvement. Check: Worked Example 39.2 gained 6 dB for a factor of four. The usual mistake is to expect 6 dB per doubling.",
}
S[40] = {
1: "Idea: the detector sees the transmit power less the coupling, and the pad must bring it below the detector's maximum. 33 − 30 = 3 dBm, so a 3 dB pad brings it to 0 dBm. Check: a 6 dB pad would leave 3 dB of headroom for peaks, often a better choice. The usual mistake is to forget the signal's peak-to-average ratio, which a peak detector sees.",
2: "Idea: the leaked wave is 10^(−(RL + D)/20) of the forward sample, and the bounds are 20 log(1 ± that). 10^(−2) = 0.01. 20 log 1.01 = +0.09 dB and 20 log 0.99 = −0.09 dB. Check: Worked Example 40.2's 35 dB gave about ±0.16 dB. The usual mistake is to forget that the bounds are set by the sum RL + D, so a good antenna match helps as much as a good coupler.",
3: "Idea: a range in decibels is the difference of the endpoints. 23 − (−40) = 63 dB. Check: 63 dB is a power ratio of two million. The usual mistake is to think the detector must cover it all; at low power, open-loop gain control usually takes over.",
4: "Idea: P(mW) = 10^(dBm/10). 10^1.5 = 31.6 mW. Check: 15 dBm is 8 dB below 23 dBm's 200 mW, and 200/6.3 = 31.6. The usual mistake is to use 10^(dBm/20).",
5: "Idea: a peak-responding diode detector charges its capacitor to the signal's peaks. OFDM's peaks are 8 to 10 dB above its average power, so the detector reads high by an amount that depends on the modulation. Check: an RMS detector or a calibrated square-law diode reads the average correctly. The usual mistake is to calibrate a peak detector with a CW signal and use it with OFDM.",
6: "Idea: a logarithmic detector produces an output voltage proportional to the logarithm of the input power, so it tracks the input power in decibels, at a fixed slope in mV/dB, over a range of 40 to 60 dB or more. Check: a 25 mV/dB slope gives 1.5 V over 60 dB. The usual mistake is to expect it to read RMS power correctly for every modulation; that is the RMS detector's job.",
}
S[41] = {
1: "Idea: efficiency follows from the DC and fundamental currents with V1 = VDD. The current Imax/2 + (Imax/2) sin ωt has IDC = Imax/2 and a fundamental amplitude I1 = Imax/2. Pout = V1I1/2 = VDD Imax/4. PDC = VDD IDC = VDD Imax/2. η = (VDD Imax/4)/(VDD Imax/2) = 50 %. Check: the DC current never falls with the signal, which is why class A's efficiency falls in proportion to output power at back-off. The usual mistake is to use a resistive load, which gives 25 %; the 50 % assumes an inductor feed.",
2: "Idea: η(α) = (α − sin α)/[4(sin(α/2) − (α/2)cos(α/2))]. α = 200° = 3.491 rad. Numerator 3.491 − (−0.342) = 3.833. Denominator 4 × (sin 100° − 1.745 cos 100°) = 4 × (0.985 + 0.303) = 5.152. η = 3.833/5.152 = 74.4 %. Check: between class B's 78.5 % at 180° and Problem 2 of Chapter 22's 60.2 % at 270°. The usual mistake is to use degrees in α − sin α.",
3: "Idea: a voltage-mode class D's fundamental is (2/π)VDD, and Pout = V1²/(2R). V1 = (2/π) × 24 = 15.3 V. Pout = 15.3²/(2 × 8) = 14.6 W. Check: (2/π)²/2 = 0.203, and 0.203 × 24²/8 = 0.203 × 72 = 14.6 W. The usual mistake is to use 4/π for a square wave from 0 to VDD; that applies to ±VDD.",
4: "Idea: class E equations: R = 0.5768 VDD²/P and C = 0.1836/(ωR). R = 0.5768 × 784/25 = 18.1 Ω. C = 0.1836/(2π × 10⁹ × 18.1) = 1.62 pF. Check: a GaN die for 25 W typically has an output capacitance of a few picofarads, so at 1 GHz this design is near its frequency limit, Section 35.2. The usual mistake is to forget the device's own Cout, which must be part of the 1.62 pF.",
5: "Idea: Ropt = (VDD − Vk)²/(2P), and class J's second harmonic is −j(3π/8)Ropt. Ropt = 11²/10 = 12.1 Ω. Z2 = −j1.178 × 12.1 = −j14.3 Ω; the fundamental is 12.1 + j12.1 Ω. Check: Worked Example 41.4 had the same ratio at 31.3 Ω. The usual mistake is to present a short at the second harmonic, which is class B, not J.",
6: "Idea: the Doherty's second efficiency peak is at 20 log(1 + r) below full power. 20 log 2.5 = 8.0 dB. Check: r = 1 gives 6.0 dB and r = 2 gives 9.5 dB. The usual mistake is to use 10 log, which gives 4 dB.",
7: "Idea: for a Rayleigh envelope, Pr{p > k⟨p⟩} = exp(−k). 4 dB is k = 10^0.4 = 2.51. exp(−2.51) = 8.1 %. Check: Section 41.9 gave 13.5 % at 3 dB and 1.9 % at 6 dB. The usual mistake is to use k = 4.",
8: "Idea: a capacitor supplying I for time t droops by ΔV = It/C, so C = It/ΔV. C = 0.5 × 2×10⁻⁶/0.25 = 4.0 µF. Check: Worked Example 41.7's 0.38 A for 1 µs with 0.2 V gave 1.9 µF. The usual mistake is to forget the DC-bias derating of a ceramic capacitor, which can take away half of its nominal value.",
9: "Idea: the worst relative supply error from misalignment is about πBΔt. For −40 dB the error must be 0.01, so Δt = 0.01/(π × 40×10⁶) = 80 ps. Check: Worked Example 41.8 found the corresponding figure for 20 MHz, twice as long. The usual mistake is to scale the alignment with the symbol time rather than the bandwidth.",
10: "Idea: with a fixed shaping table the supply voltage is a fixed function of the input envelope, so the amplifier and its modulator behave as one nonlinear system with memory. A memory polynomial can model and invert that system. An independently varying supply would be a second input the predistorter cannot see. Check: Section 41.10 makes the same argument for the hybrid modulator. The usual mistake is to retune the shaping table without retraining the predistorter.",
}
